## 4.3 Dimensional grammar

The dimensional-basis work remains one of the most important stabilizers.

Pure geometry cannot generate an SI velocity or mass without dimensional input. Candidate characteristic velocities include forms such as:

\[
v_*^2 \sim \frac{T_0}{\mu_0}
\]

or

\[
v_*^2 \sim \frac{\kappa}{\rho \ell_f^2}.
\]

These are meaningful if the input dimensions and physical roles are declared. They do not become derivations of \(c\) merely because a parameter is fitted to \(c\).

Similarly, absolute mass requires a dimensional anchor. Relative ratios may be derived dimensionlessly, but that distinction must remain explicit.

