## 4.5 Metric-induction audit

The candidate expression

\[
g_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{eff}}
=
\left(
\langle v_\mu v_\nu\rangle
\right)^{-1}
\]

does not by itself produce Lorentzian signature from Euclidean vectors.

For real Euclidean tangent vectors, the second-moment matrix is positive semidefinite. Its ordinary inverse remains positive definite wherever invertible.

A signature change therefore requires an additional declared structure, for example:

- a signed response kernel;
- a constrained Hessian with one negative mode;
- a quotient construction;
- a contact or symplectic structure;
- a clock-map that is not an ordinary metric inversion;
- an analytic continuation;
- an indefinite bilinear form induced at readout.

The transition file calls this the highest-value bottleneck, but its proposed “flow dominance” mechanism does not yet supply the missing mathematical operation. fileciteturn0file10

## 4.6 Recursive superhelix audit

The older explicit 4D superhelix uses products of sine and cosine factors across coordinate planes. fileciteturn0file17

That construction is useful as a historical executable ansatz, but it has several weaknesses:

- recursion is coordinate-specific;
- frame transport is implicit;
- invariance under reparameterization is unclear;
- the operation does not naturally encode finite core;
- multiplication of modes lacks a geometric category;
- topology and closure are not explicit.

The modern replacement should be a recursive operator acting on framed geometry.

