# 3. Changes to conceptual architecture

## 3.1 The worldtube transition is not simply “add radius”

The earlier supervisory summary correctly warned that worldtubes introduce additional structure, but Nathan’s correction matters: SAT already used thickness, bending, and interaction behavior.

The actual architectural transition is therefore better described as:

\[
\text{implicitly thickened worldline model}
\rightarrow
\text{explicitly typed finite-boundary worldtube model}.
\]

The new work is not inventing thickness. It is making explicit:

- what the thickness parameter means;
- which normal directions are materially active;
- what the exterior can interact with;
- whether the internal geometry is modeled, integrated out, or inaccessible;
- what limit recovers the earlier worldline model;
- which former “worldline” terms were already effective worldtube terms.

## 3.2 The worldline baseline must be recovered before extension

The startup source correctly identifies a major risk: building H(s)H against a remembered impression of SAT’s strongest results.

The rebuild therefore needs **Worldline Win Records** containing:

- phenomenon;
- control construction;
- geometric modification;
- derivation chain;
- target benchmark;
- holdouts;
- cross-sector consequences;
- source locations;
- current status;
- unresolved dependencies.

Without this record, a new worldtube module cannot demonstrate that it preserves the old result.

## 3.3 Lean’s role is narrower and more valuable than “formalize everything”

Lean is best suited to:

- object typing;
- parameter separation;
- namespace control;
- dimension structure;
- algebraic derivations;
- invariant propagation;
- dependency exclusion;
- formal relationships among solver operations.

It should not adjudicate whether an imported physical formalism is appropriate or whether a numerical fit is explanatory.

A particularly promising future direction is to formalize the **solver grammar**—UI, Scrollsaw, and Whirligig operations—after the executable implementation is stable. That would move Lean closer to adjudicating whether a claimed geometric translation follows the declared rules.

## 3.4 Symbol management becomes the first Lean layer

