# 2. Current conceptual architecture

## 2.1 Empirical starting surface

The current rebuild no longer treats “Euclidean \(\mathbb R^4\) full of helices” as an unquestioned foundation.

The stronger ordering is:

\[
\text{empirically constrained Minkowski grammar}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate higher-dimensional representation}
\rightarrow
\text{finite-core histories}
\rightarrow
\text{interactions}
\rightarrow
\text{readout}.
\]

This preserves the strongest methodological feature of SAT: known physical behavior constrains the admissible geometry.

## 2.2 Represented history versus H(s)H parametrization

The represented object and its chosen parametrization must remain distinct:

\[
\text{history/worldtube}
\neq
\text{H(s)H representation}.
\]

A worldtube may admit several coordinate, frame, spectral, or graphical descriptions. H(s)H is one candidate representation class, particularly suitable for nested rotation, oscillation, phase, curvature, torsion, and scale structure.

## 2.3 Recommended object hierarchy

### Core history

\[
X:I_s\times I_\tau\rightarrow\mathbb R^4.
\]

- \(s\): material or intrinsic path parameter;
- \(\tau\): history-evolution parameter.

Observed clock time \(t\), general affine parameter \(\lambda\), and scale control \(r\) must remain separate typed quantities unless a map between them is explicitly declared.

### Framed history

\[
(X,F),
\qquad
F:I_s\times I_\tau\rightarrow SO(4).
\]

The frame supplies controlled normal-direction information and transport.

### Accessible finite-core worldtube

A useful near-core state is:

\[
\mathcal W=
(X,F,A,\chi,q,\mathcal C,\mathcal B),
\]

where \(A\) represents effective finite-core or boundary data rather than automatically assuming a full metric tubular neighborhood.

This distinction matters because a regular curve in four dimensions has a three-dimensional normal space. Selecting a material two-plane for a rope-like cross-section is additional structure.

### Braided family

\[
\mathcal B=
(\mathcal W_1,\ldots,\mathcal W_N,\beta,\mathcal L,\mathcal K),
\]

where:

- \(\beta\): exchange data;
- \(\mathcal L\): linking and closure information;
- \(\mathcal K\): contact, exclusion, or collision rules.

A single projected curve does not become a braid merely because it appears intertwined.

