Worldline Recovery Lab

Its job is to reconstruct the “best of” the existing work:

- phenomenon mapped;
- control case;
- admitted modification;
- derivation chain;
- numerical result;
- benchmarks tested;
- cross-sector consequences;
- current confidence;
- exact source locations;
- unresolved dependencies.

This produces the worldline baseline specification against which the worldtube theory will be tested.

Without this step, we risk building H(s)H against a remembered impression of what worked.

### 2. Mathematical Machinery Lab

This is the math-heavy NotebookLM.

Its first job is extraction, not adaptation:

- native mathematical object;
- definitions;
- equations;
- theorem or algorithm;
- hypotheses;
- regularity conditions;
- dimensionality;
- inputs and outputs;
- invariants;
- failure cases;
- computational requirements;
- exact source location.

Only after that should it suggest which worldtube slot the machinery might serve.

Every candidate should attach to a specific gap such as:

- tube definition;
- normal-frame transport;
- cross-sectional dynamics;
- constitutive mechanics;
- resolving-sheet intersection;
- braid/contact mechanics;
- closure or holonomy;
- coarse-graining;
- stability;
- thin-tube reduction.

“Mathematically resembles SAT” is not enough.

### 3. Translation and Specification Lab

This takes a native mathematical formalism and writes the proposed SAT bridge:

| Native structure | SAT/H(s)H candidate |
|---|---|
| Domain and object | Exact worldtube object |
| Variables | SAT variables |
| Assumptions | Available/missing/incompatible |
| Governing equations | Adapted equations |
| Conserved quantities | Proposed invariants |
| Boundary conditions | SAT boundary conditions |
| Native output | Intended SAT output |
| Limiting behavior | Worldline recovery |
| New commitments | Explicit additions |

It then produces a test contract. Coding should not begin until this contract exists.

### 4. Python Experimental Lab

Python should test calculational behavior:

- numerical solutions;
- limiting cases;
- stability;
- parameter sensitivity;
- convergence;
- conservation;
- coarse-graining;
- benchmark recovery;
- modified versus unmodified controls;
- cross-sector consequences.

The generated code must be subordinate to the equation specification. It cannot quietly repair the mathematics by inserting clamps, normalization factors, target constants, or convenient initial conditions.

### 5. Formalization Lab

Lean and Python do different jobs.

Lean can establish that:

- definitions are well-typed;
- assumptions are explicit;
- inference chains follow;
- algebraic identities hold;
- invariants follow from declared premises;
- dependency chains do not silently use excluded propositions.

Lean cannot establish that:

- a mathematical object is physically appropriate;
- the source formalism was relevant;
- the model matches nature;
- a numerical coincidence is explanatory;
- an empirical benchmark was chosen fairly.

Lean is an excellent integrity lock, but not a physical adjudicator.

A staged approach probably makes sense:

1. Python/SymPy exploration.
2. Stabilized mathematical specification.
3. Lean formalization of load-bearing definitions and results.

Trying to formalize every speculative equation in Lean would fossilize noise.

