# 6. Geometric solver translations

## Historical operator

\[
\operatorname{HarmonicModulate}
:
\Theta_{\mathrm{hist}}
\ightarrow
X_{\mathrm{hist}}(s)\in\mathbb R^4.
\]

The initial round-trip fixture should use the exact third-order formula from the source, including separate phase families.

## Frame-relative nesting

\[
\operatorname{NestInFrame}
:
(X,F,P,\xi)
\ightarrow
X_{\mathrm{nested}},
\]

\[
X_{\mathrm{nested}}(s)
=
X(s)+F(s)P\xi(s).
\]

The solver must declare:

- frame convention;
- normal-plane basis;
- child radius;
- child phase;
- child winding rate;
- parameter alignment;
- closure rule.

## Holonomy

\[
\operatorname{Hol}_\gamma(\Omega)
=
\mathcal P
\exp\left(
\oint_\gamma\Omega
\ight).
\]

The implementation must specify:

- whether \(\Omega\in\mathfrak{so}(4)\) or a normal-bundle subalgebra;
- the loop \(\gamma\);
- the representation;
- multiplication order;
- frame gauge;
- comparison invariant.

## Braid

A braid object must contain multiple strands:

\[
\mathcal B
=
\left(
\mathcal G_1,\ldots,\mathcal G_N,
\beta,
\mathcal L,
\mathcal K
\ight),
\]

where:

- \(\beta\) records exchanges;
- \(\mathcal L\) records linking or closure data;
- \(\mathcal K\) records contact or collision constraints.

The historical single-curve harmonic modulation should never be classified as a braid merely because it looks intertwined under projection.

